# nano-ctrl-bindings.nanorc — a Meta-free nano keymap for browser terminals
# =========================================================================
#
# PROBLEM
#   In browser-based terminals (Apache Guacamole, cloud shells) on macOS,
#   the Option key composes special characters instead of sending Meta, so
#   every M-x shortcut in nano is unreachable. macOS also intercepts
#   Ctrl+Space (input-source switcher) and Ctrl+arrows (Mission Control),
#   which are nano's fallback word-movement keys.
#
# SOLUTION
#   Rebind the important Meta functions onto Ctrl keys whose default
#   actions are duplicated by other keys (arrows, PgUp/PgDn, Backspace) or
#   are useless in a browser terminal (suspend). Everything not bound here
#   still works via the Esc prefix: a single Esc = Meta (Esc U = M-U),
#   a double Esc = Ctrl. Tested on GNU nano 7.2.
#
# INSTALL
#   Append to your ~/.nanorc on the REMOTE (Linux) machine:
#       cat nano-ctrl-bindings.nanorc >> ~/.nanorc
#   or keep it as a separate file and include it:
#       echo 'include "~/.nano/nano-ctrl-bindings.nanorc"' >> ~/.nanorc
#   (For old nano 2.9.x — Amazon Linux 2 / EMR nodes — see the
#   compatibility note at the bottom.)
#
# THE KEYMAP
#   New key   Function                        Default key   What the Ctrl key did before (and why that's OK to lose)
#   ^Z        undo                            M-U           suspend — pointless in a browser terminal
#   ^Y        redo                            M-E           page up — PgUp key (Fn+Up on Mac) still does it
#   ^B        back one word                   M-Space       back one char — Left arrow does it
#   ^F        forward one word                ^Space        fwd one char — Right arrow does it
#   ^P        scroll up (cursor stays)        M--           prev line — Up arrow does it
#   ^N        scroll down (cursor stays)      M-+           next line — Down arrow does it
#   ^C        copy line / marked region       M-6           cursor-position report — rarely needed
#   ^H        delete word backward            M-Backspace   same as Backspace — the Backspace key itself
#                                                           sends a different code (DEL) and keeps working
#   ^J        jump to matching bracket        M-]           justify paragraph — rare when editing configs;
#                                                           still available as Esc J (full justify: Esc Esc J)
#   ^L        comment/uncomment line          M-3           refresh & center screen — cosmetic, rarely needed
#   F7 / F8   previous / next file buffer     M-< / M->     nothing (F7/F8 are unbound) — zero cost;
#                                                           works only if the browser passes F-keys through
#
# STILL AVAILABLE WITHOUT META (no rebinding needed — built in):
#   Set mark (select)      ^6         (then move; Shift+arrows often work too)
#   Cut / paste            ^K / ^U
#   Search / next match    ^W  /  ^W then Enter
#   Previous match         ^Q
#   Replace                ^\
#   Go to line             ^_         (in the prompt: ^Y = first line, ^V = last line)
#   Indent / unindent      mark with ^6, then Tab / Shift+Tab
#   Delete word forward    ^Del       (if the terminal sends it)
#   Page down              ^V         (kept deliberately, since ^Y became redo)
#
# RARE META FUNCTIONS — use the Esc prefix (single Esc = Meta):
#   Esc D = word count     Esc V = verbatim input     Esc : / Esc ; = macros
#   Esc N = line numbers   Esc P = show whitespace    Esc S = soft wrap
#   Tip: on macOS map Caps Lock -> Esc (System Settings > Keyboard >
#   Modifier Keys) and the Esc prefix becomes painless.

bind ^Z undo main
bind ^Y redo main
bind ^B prevword main
bind ^F nextword main
bind ^P scrollup main
bind ^N scrolldown main
bind ^C copy main
bind ^H chopwordleft main
bind ^J matchbracket main
bind ^L comment main
bind F7 prevbuf main
bind F8 nextbuf main

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CAVEATS
#
# * ^H / Backspace: most terminals (Guacamole included) send DEL (0x7F) for
#   the Backspace key, so binding ^H is safe. If your Backspace suddenly
#   deletes whole words, your terminal sends ^H for Backspace — delete the
#   "bind ^H chopwordleft main" line.
#
# * Page up: with ^Y taken by redo, page-up is the PgUp key only
#   (Fn+Up arrow on a Mac laptop). Page-down still has both ^V and PgDn.
#
# * OLD NANO (2.9.x — Amazon Linux 2, EMR nodes): some function names
#   differ or don't exist. Replace:
#       copy          -> copytext
#       chopwordleft  -> cutwordleft
#       matchbracket  -> (not bindable; remove the ^J line)
#   scrollup/scrolldown, comment, prevbuf/nextbuf, undo/redo work as-is.
#   An unknown name makes nano print an error at startup and ignore that
#   line — annoying but harmless.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
